      "sequence_position": 4,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14228956/assets/594326f3ca6776468b380d07fc054087e3ca7d2cb20b283206b21805a0c84385.png)\n\nWhich of the following diagrams depicts the lines representing the principal axes of the quadratic curve shown above?",
      "worked_solution": "The principal axes of a curve define a coordinate system in which the curve can be represented by its (simplest) canonical equation. \n\nOur curve is an ellipse. This curve will have the canonical equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nif the axes pass through the opposite vertices of the ellipse.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14228956/assets/542ebdbeb263bbee21edd5d07e0c0288d7ed7f577efca2a17456c6d3b9db988c.png)",
